  "reasoning": "Why (4) fails: Premises (2) and (3) only say (i) what it means for a rule to 'ignore' a criterion (the rule's outcome must be invariant to how patients scored on that criterion), and (ii) that a fair rule must not ignore any reasonable criterion. From these you can only conclude that a fair rule must be sensitive to every reasonable criterion (i.e. changing a criterion's scores can sometimes change the outcome). You cannot conclude that the rule must, in the actual profile, place first any patient who happens to be ranked first on some reasonable criterion. Sensitivity to a criterion does not imply that when that criterion singles out some patient as top, the rule must always choose that patient; the rule can weigh and aggregate multiple criteria and so be sensitive to each without making every criterion's top-ranked patient the winner. Hence the inference to (4) is invalid: it equivocates between 'the rule sometimes depends on a criterion' and 'the rule must always act so as to put that criterion's top-ranked patient first in the given profile.'",
